    {
      "kind": "prose",
      "heading": "Trees, commits and the graph",
      "body": "Names live in trees: each tree entry is the SHA-1 of a blob or subtree with its mode, type and filename [1]. A commit names the top-level tree of a snapshot and its parent commits, if any, plus author and committer [2]. Because every commit points at its parents, the commits form a directed acyclic graph [3], a graph of the same kind [[PageRank]] walks over links. Blob, tree, commit and tag are the four object types [4].",
      "sources": [
        {
          "url": "https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Internals-Git-Objects",
          "title": "Pro Git, 10.2 Git Internals - Git Objects",
          "quote": "A single tree object contains one or more entries, each of which is the SHA-1 hash of a blob or subtree with its associated mode, type, and filename."
        },
        {
          "url": "https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Internals-Git-Objects",
          "title": "Pro Git, 10.2 Git Internals - Git Objects",
          "quote": "it specifies the top-level tree for the snapshot of the project at that point; the parent commits if any"
        },
        {
          "url": "https://git-scm.com/docs/gitglossary",
          "title": "gitglossary: a Git glossary (git-scm.com)",
          "quote": "The commit objects form a directed acyclic graph, because they have parents (directed)"
        },
        {
          "url": "https://git-scm.com/docs/gitglossary",
          "title": "gitglossary: a Git glossary (git-scm.com)",
          "quote": "One of the identifiers \"commit\", \"tree\", \"tag\" or \"blob\""
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "prose",
      "heading": "A branch is a 41-byte file",
      "body": "A branch is a simple pointer or reference to the head of a line of work [1]. On disk it is a file holding the 40-character hash of a commit, so creating one means writing 41 bytes: 40 characters and a newline [2]. HEAD is usually a symbolic reference to the branch you are on [3], and the branch head moves forward as you commit [4].",
      "sources": [
        {
          "url": "https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Internals-Git-References",
          "title": "Pro Git, 10.3 Git Internals - Git References",
          "quote": "That's basically what a branch in Git is: a simple pointer or reference to the head of a line of work."
        },
        {
          "url": "https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Branching-Branches-in-a-Nutshell",
          "title": "Pro Git, 3.1 Git Branching - Branches in a Nutshell",
          "quote": "Creating a new branch is as quick and simple as writing 41 bytes to a file (40 characters and a newline)."
        },
        {
          "url": "https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Internals-Git-References",
          "title": "Pro Git, 10.3 Git Internals - Git References",
          "quote": "Usually the HEAD file is a symbolic reference to the branch you're currently on."
        },
        {
          "url": "https://git-scm.com/docs/gitglossary",
          "title": "gitglossary: a Git glossary (git-scm.com)",
          "quote": "The tip of the branch is referenced by a branch head, which moves forward as additional development is done on the branch."
        }
      ]
